/T (Pattern: A dot is inserted within the domain string, splitting it into a subdomain and a second-level domain \(e.g., 'victoria.ssecret.com'\). The browser renders this as a subdomain of 'ssecret.com', not of 'victoriassecret.com'. Users focused on the beginning of a URL \('victoria'\) may not notice the structural split. This technique is used in phishing kits to construct URLs like 'secure.login.victoria.ssecret.com'.)
/E (Pattern: A dot is inserted within the domain string, splitting it into a subdomain and a second-level domain \(e.g., 'victoria.ssecret.com'\). The browser renders this as a subdomain of 'ssecret.com', not of 'victoriassecret.com'. Users focused on the beginning of a URL \('victoria'\) may not notice the structural split. This technique is used in phishing kits to construct URLs like 'secure.login.victoria.ssecret.com'.)
